R"VG(
# news.vg (H-04; the second pass 2026-10-10): the news's register: an event as a newspaper or a broadcast told it (`@news public news`):
# a headline (its own line; the decoder writes everything in capitals), then the item: the place first, the numbers given, the officials
# quoted. The industrial age's papers and the modern age's broadcasts share it (`age` guards the words: the wire, the telephone, the
# ministry; `medium` the spoken lead of a broadcast). Each item is built from parts (a lead, a detail, a close) drawn from pools that
# carry the people's own words (their crops and crafts, their towns' quarters and landmarks, their coin), so two peoples' papers do
# not print the same sentence. news_land.vg: the land, the works, the rule; news_collapse.vg: the collapse, the sky, the quiet weeks; news_everyday.vg and
# news_everyday2.vg: the everyday (fairs, fires, storms, trials, prices...).

@root news.*

# ---- who said it -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

news.said
  {news.who} {news.saidv}
news.who
  (gov=monarchy) a spokesman for the palace
  (gov=monarchy) an officer of the court
  (gov=chiefs) one of the elders
  (gov=council) a member of the council
  (gov=council) the clerk to the council
  (gov=theocracy) a priest of {holyplace}
  (gov=theocracy) the temple's secretary
  (gov=republic) a spokesman for the consuls
  (gov=republic) an under-secretary
  (gov=assembly) a delegate for {$home}
  (gov=corporate) a spokesman for the companies
  (gov=corporate) a director who asked not to be named
  (age>=modern) a ministry spokesman
  (age>=modern) a government source
  an official at {$seat}
  the town clerk of {$home}
  the keeper of the seals
  a secretary to the council
news.saidv
  said
  told this paper
  insisted
  admitted
  announced
  assured our correspondent
  let it be known
  conceded

# where the news came from
news.wire
  (inv.telegraph=1) by wire
  (inv.telephone=1) by telephone
  (inv.radio=1) by radio
  by the morning post
  (inv.railway=1) by the night train
  by a rider from the coast road
  from travellers

# ---- war ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

news.war
  {news.h.war}\n{news.war.m} {news.war.x}
  (medium=printed) {news.h.war}\n{news.war.m} {news.war.x} {news.war.t}
news.h.war
  War
  War declared
  The war
  ($e.warkind=other) War with the {other}
  ($e.warkind=conquest) Invasion
  ($e.warkind=rebellion) {$e.place} in revolt
  ($e.warkind=revolution) Revolution
  ($e.warkind=civil|$e.warkind=succession) Civil war
  (medium!=printed) This is the news
news.war.m
  ($e.warkind=other) War has been declared on the {other} [by order of {ruler.the}|after the failure of the talks|after the border shootings of {season.this}|after the burning of the frontier posts].
  ($e.warkind=other) The {other} have been told that a state of war exists between their people and ours, and [their envoys have been sent home|their traders have been given three days to leave|the frontier roads are closed].
  ($e.warkind=conquest) The {other} have crossed the frontier in force; {news.said} that [our forces are falling back in good order|the border towns are being emptied|the army has been called up in full].
  ($e.warkind=rebellion) {$e.place} has declared itself free of {ruler.the} and [closed its gates|seized the treasury|hanged the tax collectors in effigy|raised its own flag].
  ($e.warkind=rebellion has:$e.place.landmark) The rebels at {$e.place} have raised their flag over {$e.place.landmark} and say they will not take it down.
  ($e.warkind=rebellion) The town of {$e.place} is in open revolt; [the garrison has gone over to the rebels|the governor has fled|the bridges have been pulled down].
  ($e.warkind=rebellion has:$e.place.quarter) Barricades stand in {$e.place.quarter} of {$e.place}, and the town has declared for itself.
  ($e.warkind=revolution) Crowds at {$e.place} have seized the public buildings and declared the old order at an end.
  ($e.warkind=revolution has:$e.ruler) The rule of {$e.ruler:name} is said to be over at {$e.place}, where [committees|the workers' councils|the students and the dockers] now sit in the [council house|town hall|post office].
  ($e.warkind=civil|$e.warkind=succession|$e.warkind=cities|$e.warkind=bands) Fighting has broken out over {$e.place}, [and there is no sign of a truce|and both sides claim the right|and the roads out of the town are full].
  ($e.warkind=succession) Two claimants now call themselves the rightful heir, and the provinces are choosing between them.
  ($e.warkind=cities) The quarrel between {$e.place} and its neighbours, long a matter for the courts, has become a matter for the army.
news.war.x
  The army is said to be ready, and [the {herd} of the border farms have been driven inland|the bakers of {$home} have been told to bake for the regiments|the {craft} works at {$home} are to make for the army until further notice].
  (has:$home.landmark) Recruiting tables have been set up in {town.open} of every town, and the queue at {$home} reached {$home.landmark} by noon.
  Recruiting tables have been set up in {town.open} of every town, and the queues were longer than the bread queues.
  The price of {crop} rose by a third on the news, and [{drink}|salt|lamp oil] is to be rationed from {season.next}.
  (age>=modern) Reservists have been ordered to report by the end of the week, and the railway timetables are suspended.
  Volunteers were drilling in {town.open} at {$home} by evening, [some with rifles and some with brooms|in their work clothes|to the sound of {instrument}].
  The schools at {$home} will close early so that the older children can help with the harvest.
  The families of soldiers will receive half pay in {coin}, the council promises, from the first of the month.
  (inv.telegraph=1) The telegraph offices were open all night, and [still could not keep up|the clerks slept at their desks|the lines to the border were reserved for the army].
news.war.t
  The markets fell at the news, {crop} first and {craft} goods after.
  {news.said} that the war would be short, as they always do.
  {news.said} that there was no cause for alarm, which caused alarm.
  (has:gazette) {gazette} urges calm, and patience, and the buying of war bonds at {n} {coin}.
  Prayers for victory will be said at {holyplace} on the rest day, and {news.rite.n}.
  The {festival} fair is cancelled, and the {ind.sport} with it.
  In {town.open} at {$home} people stood about {time.of} waiting for more news, and none came.
  The {colour.holy} flags are out in {streets.of}, and nobody remembers putting them there.

news.battle
  {news.h.battle}\n{news.battle.m} {news.battle.x}
  (medium=printed) {news.h.battle}\n{news.battle.m} {news.battle.x} {news.battle.t}
news.h.battle
  ($e.won=1) Victory at {$e.place}
  ($e.won=1) {$e.place}: the enemy routed
  ($e.won=0) Defeat at {$e.place}
  ($e.won=0) Grave news from {$e.place}
  ($e.won=draw) Battle at {$e.place}
  ($e.won=draw) No decision at {$e.place}
news.battle.m
  ($e.won=1) Our forces held the field at {$e.place} after [a day|two days|a night and a day] of fighting, with losses put at {#$e.value:about}.
  ($e.won=1) The enemy broke at {$e.place} [in the afternoon|at first light|when the reserves came up], leaving {#$e.value:about} dead on both sides and [the baggage|their guns|their standards] in our hands.
  ($e.won=1 has:$e.a) {$e.a:full} has won a great victory at {$e.place}; the dead are put at {#$e.value:about}.
  ($e.won=0) Reports from {$e.place} speak of a heavy defeat; the losses are put at {#$e.value:about}, and [the army is falling back|the town has been given up|the survivors are coming home by every road].
  ($e.won=0) Our army has been beaten at {$e.place}, {news.beaten.how}. {news.said} that [the men fought well|the enemy had the advantage of numbers|the orders came too late], and that {#$e.value:about} have died.
  ($e.won=draw) Both sides claim the field at {$e.place}. The dead are put at {#$e.value:about}.
  ($e.won=draw) The armies met at {$e.place} and parted again at nightfall, neither giving way; {#$e.value:about} are dead.
news.battle.x
  The lists of the fallen will be posted at the town halls [from tomorrow|as they arrive|on the doors of {holyplace}].
  (inv.telegraph=1) Further news is expected {news.wire} tonight, and crowds are waiting outside the offices of this paper.
  The wounded are being brought [by cart|by river|by the night post] to {$home}, where {houses} have been opened to them.
  ($e.won=1) The bells of {$home} were rung until the ringers were tired, and {drink} flowed free at the inns.
  ($e.won=0) The mood at {$home} is [quiet|grim|angry], and nobody went to the {festival} rehearsal.
  The women of {$home} are cutting up old {garment.n:pl} for bandages in {town.open}.
  The field at {$e.place} is said to be [all mud|burned black|trampled to dust] for a mile around.
news.battle.t
  The fighting is said to be heavy, and the {herd} of the farms round it lost.
  {news.said} that the war is going as planned, though not whose plan.
  (has:$e.b) The enemy was led by {$e.b:full}, of whom more is said than is known.
  It is too early to say what this means for the war; the {crop} will have to be got in either way.
  The families of {$home} are reading the lists in {town.open} {time.of}.

news.siege
  ($e.out=1) {$e.place} has fallen\nAfter [weeks|months|a long winter] under siege, {$e.place} has opened its gates. {news.siege.t}
  ($e.out!=1) {$e.place} besieged\n{$e.place} is surrounded; [the last road out was cut on {season.now}'s first day|no carts have gone in for a month|the garrison has refused to talk]. {news.siege.t}
news.siege.t
  Inside the walls, it is said, they are down to [a ration a day|half a ration|what the {herd} were eating].
  {news.said} that the town would hold [until relieved|as long as it has water|for a year if it must].
  Refugees from the villages round {$e.place} are sleeping in the fields along the road, under {garment.n:pl}.
  The {watersrc.n} inside the walls is said to be still sweet, which is something.
  The besiegers have dug in on the {crop} fields, and burned what they could not eat.

news.sack
  {$e.place} sacked\nThe town of {$e.place} has been put to the sword; {#$e.value:about} are said to be dead and {news.sack.t}
news.sack.t
  the fires near the market are burning still.
  the survivors are on the roads with what they could carry, {herd} and {vessel} and children.
  not one {vessel.one.n} was left in the market, our correspondent writes.
  the bells of {$e.place} have been carried off, and {holyplace} burned.

news.peace
  {news.h.peace}\n{news.peace.m} {news.peace.t}
news.h.peace
  Peace
  The war is over
  Peace signed
  ($e.won=1) Peace with honour
  ($e.won=0) Peace at a price
news.peace.m
  (has:$e.war) {$e.war} is over. The terms will be published in full tomorrow.
  (has:$e.war) The treaty that ends {$e.war} was signed at {$e.place} [at noon yesterday|after three days of talks|in the small hours].
  ($e.won=1) The enemy has accepted our terms, and the army will be home before {season.next}.
  ($e.won=0) The terms are hard, {news.said}, but the war is over and the men are coming home.
  ($e.won=draw) Neither side has won and both have stopped, which may be the best that could be had.
news.peace.t
  Crowds gathered in the squares of {city} through the night, [singing|weeping|lighting lamps in the windows].
  The bells of every town were rung at once, and the {bird} rose off the roofs of {$home}.
  There will be a holiday on the rest day, and the council has promised to pay for the {drink}.
  The prisoners are to be sent home by [the coast road|the river|the first boats], with a {vessel.one.n} of {food} each.
  Nobody at {$home} quite believes it yet, and the {colour.mourn} has not come down from the doors.
  The {craft} works will go back to making what they made before.
news.beaten.how
  and the {herd} of the baggage train lost with it
  with the {colour.holy} standards taken
  in {weather.now}
  after holding the field until noon
)VG"
